your Wiimote or whatever.

00:59:20.010 --> 00:59:22.380
- You guys sit in your
comfort of knowing what works.

00:59:22.380 --> 00:59:25.120
I am the daring type,
who tried something new.

00:59:25.120 --> 00:59:26.880
I wanted to see if I could get an egg

00:59:26.880 --> 00:59:27.713
to cook on a burger bun.

00:59:27.713 --> 00:59:29.050
- I knew it wasn't gonna go well

00:59:29.050 --> 00:59:30.690
because the whole ordeal started

00:59:30.690 --> 00:59:32.913
with Connor going, "Watch this."

00:59:32.913 --> 00:59:36.210
Anything that starts with
a confident "watch this"

00:59:36.210 --> 00:59:37.077
five beers in, is not going to go well.

00:59:37.077 --> 00:59:39.480
- "Hold my beer." Imagine
if it worked, though.

00:59:39.480 --> 00:59:40.890
I'd have been a fucking god.

00:59:40.890 --> 00:59:41.733
- I would've applauded you.

00:59:41.733 --> 00:59:43.520
- I would've been a god amongst men.

00:59:43.520 --> 00:59:44.353
- Yes.

00:59:44.353 --> 00:59:45.186
- If I made that work, I would've.

00:59:45.186 --> 00:59:46.019
That would've been amazing.

00:59:46.019 --> 00:59:48.290
But like Icarus, I flew
too close to the sun.

00:59:48.290 --> 00:59:50.279
I tried something that I shouldn't have.

00:59:50.279 --> 00:59:51.112
What can I say?

00:59:51.112 --> 00:59:53.770
(Garnt and Joey laughing)

00:59:53.770 --> 00:59:55.620
- Then you gave up and just
like flipped the burger,

00:59:55.620 --> 00:59:58.079
and then the rest of the
egg slid into the fire.

00:59:58.079 --> 01:00:00.310
- I was like, "If I can't have the egg

01:00:00.310 --> 01:00:01.430
cook perfectly on the bun,

01:00:01.430 --> 01:00:03.760
I'll at least have this
French toast flavor

01:00:03.760 --> 01:00:06.500
to add to my burger.

01:00:06.500 --> 01:00:08.200
It added the flavor profile

01:00:08.200 --> 01:00:09.033
that I was quite happy with.

01:00:09.033 --> 01:00:12.377
- Suffice it to say that
the dinner went well.

01:00:12.377 --> 01:00:14.197
Then, under the stars,
it was amazing starlight.

01:00:14.197 --> 01:00:15.427
- [Garnt] View was amazing.

01:00:15.427 --> 01:00:17.006
- Mount Fuji in the background.

01:00:17.006 --> 01:00:17.839
We had a good time.

01:00:17.839 --> 01:00:18.672
- There was a problem, though.

01:00:18.672 --> 01:00:21.590
We were running out of wood,
and the store had closed.

01:00:21.590 --> 01:00:22.840
- I was going to say,

01:00:22.840 --> 01:00:25.798
so we ate like kings
and we drank like kings.

01:00:25.798 --> 01:00:27.990
We learned from the last camping trip.

01:00:27.990 --> 01:00:32.400
We overstocked on pretty
much every fucking resource

01:00:32.400 --> 01:00:33.233
we could think of.

01:00:33.233 --> 01:00:35.150
- Food we did perfectly.

01:00:35.150 --> 01:00:36.650
- We had a bunch of food left.

01:00:36.650 --> 01:00:37.626
- We still had a bunch of food left.

01:00:37.626 --> 01:00:38.815
- I thought we ate it all.

01:00:38.815 --> 01:00:40.200
- I ate it while you were sleeping.

01:00:40.200 --> 01:00:42.278
- If somebody had told
me, I would've ate it all.

01:00:42.278 --> 01:00:43.111
(Joey laughing)

01:00:43.111 --> 01:00:44.365
- I would have been done with it.

01:00:44.365 --> 01:00:47.287
- It's because we ran out of coal,

01:00:47.287 --> 01:00:48.973
and we ran out of firewood.

01:00:50.160 --> 01:00:52.920
In camping outside on a winter's night,

01:00:52.920 --> 01:00:56.040
we ran out of the one
resource that is essential

01:00:56.040 --> 01:00:58.870
for basically human
survival, which is heat.

01:00:58.870 --> 01:01:00.227
- It kind of sucked, 'cause it was like,

01:01:00.227 --> 01:01:02.780
"Oh, I'm pretty sure we
could still keep going."

01:01:02.780 --> 01:01:05.310
But it got to about 11
PM and we could see then.

01:01:05.310 --> 01:01:06.430
- No, no. I remember.

01:01:06.430 --> 01:01:07.991
It got to about 9 PM.

01:01:07.991 --> 01:01:09.717
I remember 'cause it got to about 9 PM

01:01:09.717 --> 01:01:12.210
and we still wanted to
cook some more food,

01:01:12.210 --> 01:01:15.640
but the wood just wasn't
bringing enough heat

01:01:15.640 --> 01:01:16.860
to actually cook stuff,

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:19.128
because it was just charring
it and then going out,

01:01:19.128 --> 01:01:20.710
'cause we needed coal for it.

01:01:20.710 --> 01:01:21.543
So we decided to stop cooking

01:01:21.543 --> 01:01:24.660
and just keep on drinking, as you do.

01:01:24.660 --> 01:01:27.171
Keep going with the liquid dinner.

01:01:27.171 --> 01:01:29.260
I remember it hit about 9 PM

01:01:29.260 --> 01:01:31.280
and it started getting a bit nippy,

01:01:31.280 --> 01:01:33.420
it started going from a
comfortable temperature

01:01:33.420 --> 01:01:35.900
to, "Okay, now I gotta
put on this winter jacket

01:01:35.900 --> 01:01:37.721
because I'm actually
starting to feel this cold."

01:01:37.721 --> 01:01:40.720
- Yeah. I went down to around
two, three degrees Celsius.

01:01:40.720 --> 01:01:41.553
- Yeah. Yeah.

01:01:41.553 --> 01:01:43.420
Yeah. Then I remember
looking at the firewood.

01:01:44.310 --> 01:01:49.010
We had somehow gone through
about 70% of the firewood

01:01:49.010 --> 01:01:51.140
that we had bought previously.

01:01:51.140 --> 01:01:52.557
I remember thinking,

01:01:52.557 --> 01:01:57.110
"Fuck. How much longer is
this going to last us?"

01:01:57.110 --> 01:01:58.023
I remember it was at that point

01:01:58.023 --> 01:02:02.720
that I think we subconsciously
started to ration it,

01:02:02.720 --> 01:02:04.870
because it got colder,

01:02:04.870 --> 01:02:06.310
but I remember the fires

01:02:06.310 --> 01:02:07.860
also started to get colder as well,

01:02:07.860 --> 01:02:10.212
'cause we weren't putting in the wood

01:02:10.212 --> 01:02:12.190
all willy nilly anymore.

01:02:12.190 --> 01:02:13.480
- I noticed that people

01:02:13.480 --> 01:02:16.831
were slowly approaching the
fire pit, closer and closer.

01:02:16.831 --> 01:02:18.640
- I was further away, initially.

01:02:18.640 --> 01:02:20.110
'Cause I was like, "I
don't need that much.

01:02:20.110 --> 01:02:20.960
I'm fine. I'm hot."

01:02:20.960 --> 01:02:22.057
But then got to the
point where I was like,

01:02:22.057 --> 01:02:24.410
"Oh, I need the fire or I'm
actually gonna just die."

01:02:24.410 --> 01:02:26.463
- Yeah. Our knees were
basically in the fire.

01:02:26.463 --> 01:02:29.550
- Made me realize how shitty fires are.

01:02:29.550 --> 01:02:31.277
They must have had to
literally sleep next to it,

01:02:31.277 --> 01:02:33.382
'cause I'm like, "This shit is awful.

01:02:33.382 --> 01:02:36.220
The range of this fire is terrible."

01:02:36.220 --> 01:02:37.620
Air is a terrible insulator.

01:02:38.550 --> 01:02:39.810
But I was like, "This is terrible.

01:02:39.810 --> 01:02:41.480
I have to basically have
my hands in this thing

01:02:41.480 --> 01:02:42.504
to be warm."

01:02:42.504 --> 01:02:44.600
- I can't believe Nature
nerfed the AoE of fire.

01:02:44.600 --> 01:02:45.610
- Can they fix that patch?

01:02:45.610 --> 01:02:47.940
Can I have the range increase of fire?

01:02:47.940 --> 01:02:49.214
It was not good.

01:02:49.214 --> 01:02:50.890
- Yeah. I dunno what
fucking Newton was thinking.

01:02:50.890 --> 01:02:52.771
What's up with that? What's up?

01:02:52.771 --> 01:02:53.663
- You could try wind nerfing the AoE.

01:02:53.663 --> 01:02:54.500
It's too powerful.

01:02:54.500 --> 01:02:57.380
- I remember the fire was hot
enough, being close to it,

01:02:57.380 --> 01:02:59.570
but then my ass was freezing fucking cold.

01:02:59.570 --> 01:03:00.403
- Oh yeah.

01:03:00.403 --> 01:03:02.970
- So every so often, I'd
just have to put my ass

01:03:02.970 --> 01:03:04.520
on top of the fire.

01:03:04.520 --> 01:03:06.490
Joey was just farting nonstop

01:03:06.490 --> 01:03:08.600
throughout the entire camping trip.

01:03:08.600 --> 01:03:10.670
Every time he put his ass across the fire,

01:03:10.670 --> 01:03:12.300
I'm like, "Joey, please
don't fart right now."

01:03:12.300 --> 01:03:17.030
I held my breath, 'cause
I knew if Joey farted,

01:03:17.030 --> 01:03:18.400
I swear to God, it would've used up

01:03:18.400 --> 01:03:19.583
the rest of the firewood.

01:03:20.658 --> 01:03:22.252
- The fire just starts to...

01:03:22.252 --> 01:03:26.750
- No. No. First of all, when
you stand up for a moment,

01:03:26.750 --> 01:03:28.820
the chair that you've been
warming up for 30 minutes

01:03:28.820 --> 01:03:30.360
instantly becomes cold again.

01:03:30.360 --> 01:03:31.360
- It instantly freezes.

01:03:31.360 --> 01:03:33.390
- Which sucks. Which meant
you didn't wanna move,

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:34.223
but you had to pee.

01:03:34.223 --> 01:03:36.450
You had to also put stuff
on the fire or whatever.

01:03:36.450 --> 01:03:38.010
So it was awful.

01:03:38.010 --> 01:03:40.807
Also, one thing that
happened where I was like,

01:03:40.807 --> 01:03:41.890
"We're gonna die,"

01:03:41.890 --> 01:03:44.710
is that they try to
light the fire starters

01:03:44.710 --> 01:03:46.360
with a gas fire.

01:03:46.360 --> 01:03:47.810
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:03:47.810 --> 01:03:49.010
(Joey laughing)

01:03:49.010 --> 01:03:49.843
- [Garnt] I remember that.

01:03:49.843 --> 01:03:51.020
- So they tried to light,

01:03:51.020 --> 01:03:53.723
one of the guys turned
on the gas for this,

01:03:53.723 --> 01:03:55.463
what would you even call it?

01:03:55.463 --> 01:03:56.296
- [Garnt] Blowtorch.

01:03:56.296 --> 01:03:57.203
- Propane torch.

01:03:57.203 --> 01:03:58.036
- Blowtorch.

01:03:58.036 --> 01:03:59.292
I was like, "Why the fuck
do we have a blowtorch?

01:03:59.292 --> 01:04:01.043
Why do we have something
like a normal lighter?"

01:04:01.043 --> 01:04:01.876
- Like a match.

01:04:01.876 --> 01:04:04.750
- I don't know. But we had
a blowtorch, and he lit it,

01:04:04.750 --> 01:04:06.720
and he went to go and
point it to start a fire,

01:04:06.720 --> 01:04:08.810
and the whole blowtorch just went on fire.

01:04:08.810 --> 01:04:11.547
I was like, "Uh. You
might wanna put that out."

01:04:12.682 --> 01:04:13.840
The torch was catching on fire,

01:04:13.840 --> 01:04:15.020
the gas canister's right next to it.

01:04:15.020 --> 01:04:17.930
I'm like "Uh-oh. Uh-oh.
Fire plus can pressure.

01:04:17.930 --> 01:04:19.442
Uh-oh. Uh-oh."

01:04:19.442 --> 01:04:20.510
And I was like, "I'm getting away.

01:04:20.510 --> 01:04:22.733
What the fuck? Put it out! Put it out!"

01:04:22.733 --> 01:04:24.283
- But the thing is, we
were six or seven people.

01:04:24.283 --> 01:04:25.597
Everybody else is just
kind of sitting there like,

01:04:25.597 --> 01:04:26.940
"Oh, it's on fire."

01:04:26.940 --> 01:04:28.983
- I remember that exact moment.

01:04:29.830 --> 01:04:31.410
- I backed off. I was running away.

01:04:31.410 --> 01:04:33.167
People are just sitting there watching,

01:04:33.167 --> 01:04:35.023
not helping at all,

01:04:35.023 --> 01:04:38.553
just watching this guy holding
this gas canister on fire.

01:04:38.553 --> 01:04:40.464
I'm like, "I'm running away."

01:04:40.464 --> 01:04:42.080
I'm like, "What the fuck
are you guys doing? Run!"

01:04:42.080 --> 01:04:42.913
- Yeah.

01:04:43.960 --> 01:04:45.620
I remember my exact POV was

01:04:45.620 --> 01:04:46.720
I see it happen.

01:04:46.720 --> 01:04:48.640
I see him pointing it towards the fire.

01:04:48.640 --> 01:04:51.926
He presses the button and
then the gas just leaks

01:04:51.926 --> 01:04:53.790
throughout the entire can.

01:04:53.790 --> 01:04:55.560
So the entire can is just covered.

01:04:55.560 --> 01:04:57.930
Connor goes "fight or flight" mode.

01:04:57.930 --> 01:05:00.000
He's just like when that one quiet kid

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:01.120
reached for his bag, you know.

01:05:01.120 --> 01:05:03.854
He's half a mile down.

01:05:03.854 --> 01:05:05.363
- [Connor] I'm not liking this.

01:05:05.363 --> 01:05:08.314
- Me, being six cans of
beers in, I'm just like,

01:05:08.314 --> 01:05:09.853
"Oh, fire."

01:05:09.853 --> 01:05:11.970
(all laughing)

01:05:11.970 --> 01:05:13.507
- It was only after Connor's like,

01:05:13.507 --> 01:05:16.507
"Guys, what the fuck are you
doing? We almost just died!"

01:05:16.507 --> 01:05:19.530
And I'm just like, "But
the fire. It's so pretty."

01:05:19.530 --> 01:05:20.870
- [Joey] Yeah.

01:05:20.870 --> 01:05:23.250
He was holding fire. That's so cool.

01:05:23.250 --> 01:05:24.620
- These guys didn't even say anything.

01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:27.420
They literally sat there like
stoners, just watching this.

01:05:27.420 --> 01:05:31.260
- I literally saw him
holding this can of fire,

01:05:31.260 --> 01:05:33.786
and I was like (laughs).

01:05:33.786 --> 01:05:35.236
- The guy who was holding it,

01:05:36.244 --> 01:05:37.202
(Joey laughs)

01:05:37.202 --> 01:05:39.408
- I can't remember who was holding it,

01:05:39.408 --> 01:05:40.620
but I was like, "Turn it
off! Turn the gas off!"

01:05:40.620 --> 01:05:42.050
- Yeah. Wayne was holding it.

01:05:42.050 --> 01:05:43.150
Our friend Wayne was holding it.

01:05:43.150 --> 01:05:44.313
He was like, "Oh."

01:05:45.370 --> 01:05:46.420
- He didn't what to do.

01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:47.253
- "What should I do?"

01:05:47.253 --> 01:05:48.587
- I'm like, "Turn it off! Turn it off!"

01:05:50.470 --> 01:05:52.070
- There was about to be an ass...

01:05:52.070 --> 01:05:54.410
- I ran to a distance and then
I was giving instructions.

01:05:54.410 --> 01:05:55.910
I was like, "Now turn it off!"

01:05:56.920 --> 01:05:58.220
- I remember you running to a distance,

01:05:58.220 --> 01:06:00.061
and you're like, "Guys, what
the fuck are you doing?!

01:06:00.061 --> 01:06:01.240
What are you doing?"

01:06:01.240 --> 01:06:02.110
I'm just like, "Oh yeah,

01:06:02.110 --> 01:06:04.787
I should probably care for my life."

01:06:04.787 --> 01:06:07.336
I remember thinking that
and I'm just like, "Nah."

01:06:07.336 --> 01:06:09.794
- [Connor] Either run or
help. You have two options.

01:06:09.794 --> 01:06:10.627
- Should be all right.
Should be all right.

01:06:10.627 --> 01:06:13.563
- So, yeah. Don't do what we did.

01:06:15.010 --> 01:06:17.167
Connor did the correct thing and panicked.

01:06:17.167 --> 01:06:18.000
- I could have helped,

01:06:18.000 --> 01:06:19.550
but also I would've had
to run around the fire

01:06:19.550 --> 01:06:20.542
and I was like,

01:06:20.542 --> 01:06:21.720
"I don't know how dangerous this is."

01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:25.040
My guess would've been, "How
about we leave it on the floor

01:06:25.040 --> 01:06:26.310
and then all run."

01:06:26.310 --> 01:06:27.490
- Yeah.

01:06:27.490 --> 01:06:29.560
- So that happened.

01:06:29.560 --> 01:06:31.230
I can't remember if anything else happened

01:06:31.230 --> 01:06:33.190
during the campsite.

01:06:33.190 --> 01:06:34.910
- I'm pretty sure these
gas cans have fail-safes

01:06:34.910 --> 01:06:37.191
so that even if they somehow
light on fire like that,

01:06:37.191 --> 01:06:38.024
they don't...

01:06:38.024 --> 01:06:40.367
- That's why I was so calm.

01:06:40.367 --> 01:06:42.000
- But also I know that you can't

01:06:42.000 --> 01:06:43.960
heat up a metal gas canister for very long

01:06:43.960 --> 01:06:44.793
before it goes boom.

01:06:44.793 --> 01:06:46.030
- Yeah. That's true.

01:06:46.030 --> 01:06:47.800
- I'm sure the campsite
would've loved that.

01:06:47.800 --> 01:06:49.156
- Well, we were all right.

01:06:49.156 --> 01:06:50.670
- We survived.

01:06:50.670 --> 01:06:51.750
- I don't know how we survived.

01:06:51.750 --> 01:06:53.880
- We didn't end up on r/WatchPeopleDie.

01:06:53.880 --> 01:06:55.459
So don't worry. We're all good.

01:06:55.459 --> 01:06:57.790
- All it took was a few
shakes and a bit of a blow.

01:06:57.790 --> 01:06:59.020
It wasn't a big fire,

01:06:59.020 --> 01:07:02.230
but it was just fire plus
gas canister, not good.

01:07:02.230 --> 01:07:03.852
- Yeah. That could have ended very badly.

01:07:03.852 --> 01:07:06.510
- Under any other circumstance,

01:07:06.510 --> 01:07:08.830
I would've been in the same panic mode,

01:07:08.830 --> 01:07:10.370
but kind of dunno why.

01:07:10.370 --> 01:07:13.540
I was drunk, okay? I was very drunk.

01:07:13.540 --> 01:07:15.080
- [Joey] Shockingly calm.

01:07:15.080 --> 01:07:16.880
It was like, "Just put it out.

01:07:16.880 --> 01:07:17.730
Just put it out."

01:07:18.894 --> 01:07:21.247
- I remember the thought
going through my mind of,

01:07:21.247 --> 01:07:23.110
"I should get up and run,

01:07:23.110 --> 01:07:25.850
but the seat's so comfy right now

01:07:25.850 --> 01:07:27.570
and it's really cold outside."

01:07:27.570 --> 01:07:29.550
It was like your mom asking
you to get out of bed

01:07:29.550 --> 01:07:31.030
when it's like 7:00 AM.

01:07:31.030 --> 01:07:32.253
It is how people die.

01:07:32.253 --> 01:07:33.954
- [Connor] Why people die in house fires.

01:07:33.954 --> 01:07:35.830
- It is how people die. I was very stupid.

01:07:35.830 --> 01:07:39.190
But at the time, it just felt
like the right thing to do,

01:07:39.190 --> 01:07:41.020
in my mind. Which it wasn't. It wasn't.

01:07:41.020 --> 01:07:42.060
I was very dumb.

01:07:42.060 --> 01:07:43.370
- We could have died multiple times.

01:07:43.370 --> 01:07:44.960
This is like a double
entendre, this title.

01:07:44.960 --> 01:07:46.060
- Yeah, exactly.

01:07:46.060 --> 01:07:47.958
- We almost died. We actually did.

01:07:47.958 --> 01:07:50.330
- So then that happened

01:07:50.330 --> 01:07:53.261
and then it got to around to 11 PM?

01:07:53.261 --> 01:07:56.573
- [Garnt] 11 PM? I could
have still kept going.

01:07:56.573 --> 01:07:57.601
- [Connor] Yeah, me too.

01:07:57.601 --> 01:07:58.434
I wanted to keep going.

01:07:58.434 --> 01:08:00.300
- But it gets to the point where,

01:08:00.300 --> 01:08:02.960
my entire life I've been
conditioned to think

01:08:02.960 --> 01:08:04.470
that no matter how cold it gets,

01:08:04.470 --> 01:08:07.390
I can just drink more beer
and it will solve the problem.

01:08:07.390 --> 01:08:08.400
This was the one time

01:08:08.400 --> 01:08:10.550
when I was outside with a thick jacket on

01:08:10.550 --> 01:08:12.870
and drinking more beer
didn't solve the problem.

01:08:12.870 --> 01:08:14.473
Because I just kept getting colder.

01:08:14.473 --> 01:08:15.970
I'm just like, "What's going on?"

01:08:15.970 --> 01:08:17.720
So I start trying to drink more beer.

01:08:17.720 --> 01:08:18.820
I just kept getting colder.

01:08:18.820 --> 01:08:20.661
I'm just like, "What is this?"

01:08:20.661 --> 01:08:21.494
- Well, the thing with alcohol

01:08:21.494 --> 01:08:22.922
is that you don't actually get warmer,

01:08:22.922 --> 01:08:24.490
but your body just thinks you're warmer.

01:08:24.490 --> 01:08:25.710
- [Garnt] Yeah, you just
think you're getting warmer.

01:08:25.710 --> 01:08:27.450
That's just a testament to how cold

01:08:27.450 --> 01:08:28.810
it was actually getting

01:08:28.810 --> 01:08:30.010
rather than how cold

01:08:30.010 --> 01:08:31.700
my mind was thinking it was getting.

01:08:31.700 --> 01:08:34.780
- At a point, I just wanted
to stop drinking the beer,

01:08:34.780 --> 01:08:36.050
because I would pick it up,

01:08:36.050 --> 01:08:38.570
and it would just
literally be freezing cold.

01:08:38.570 --> 01:08:40.220
- It was not even nice to drink

01:08:40.220 --> 01:08:41.670
'cause of how cold it was,

01:08:41.670 --> 01:08:43.202
'cause outside was so cold.

01:08:43.202 --> 01:08:44.090
- Would've been great in the summer.

01:08:44.090 --> 01:08:45.431
- Oh, in the summer, I'd have loved it.

01:08:45.431 --> 01:08:46.610
- It would've been amazing.

01:08:46.610 --> 01:08:49.330
But when it's like zero degrees outside

01:08:49.330 --> 01:08:51.110
and you're drinking zero-degree beer,

01:08:51.110 --> 01:08:51.943
it's not that great.

01:08:51.943 --> 01:08:52.776
- It was one of the few times

01:08:52.776 --> 01:08:53.990
where you drink a beer and it gets colder

01:08:53.990 --> 01:08:55.320
the longer you drink it.

01:08:55.320 --> 01:08:56.940
(all laughing)

01:08:56.940 --> 01:08:57.791
- It's fucking awful.

01:08:57.791 --> 01:09:00.170
- The beers would, 'cause
they're in a crate,

01:09:00.170 --> 01:09:01.080
the beers would start off

01:09:01.080 --> 01:09:02.730
at a reasonable amount of coldness

01:09:02.730 --> 01:09:05.040
and then they would get
to a reasonable coldness

01:09:05.040 --> 01:09:07.600
to ice cold to almost frozen.

01:09:07.600 --> 01:09:10.180
- Also, the thing about
ice cold beers that's nice

01:09:10.180 --> 01:09:11.360
is that you're not normally drinking it

01:09:11.360 --> 01:09:12.710
in ice-cold conditions.

01:09:12.710 --> 01:09:13.543
- [Garnt] Yeah, exactly.

01:09:13.543 --> 01:09:14.376
- So it warms up.

01:09:14.376 --> 01:09:17.277
It's a nice temperature
and it's nice in your body.

01:09:17.277 --> 01:09:18.950
"Oh, this is refreshing."

01:09:18.950 --> 01:09:21.180
But when it's ice cold
in ice-cold conditions,

01:09:21.180 --> 01:09:22.837
you're like, "This feels like a bad idea."

01:09:22.837 --> 01:09:23.826
- It's torture.

01:09:23.826 --> 01:09:24.870
- "This feels like a bad combination."

01:09:24.870 --> 01:09:27.670
Perhaps lukewarm beer would've
been better, or hot beer.

01:09:27.670 --> 01:09:28.680
That's why they drink hot wine

01:09:28.680 --> 01:09:30.100
at the Christmas festivals.

01:09:30.100 --> 01:09:32.320
- We did everything we
could to elongate this fire,

01:09:32.320 --> 01:09:33.760
but nothing was working.

01:09:33.760 --> 01:09:37.153
There was only so much we
could ration out this firewood.

01:09:37.153 --> 01:09:41.290
I think the last log
lasted us for a full hour.

01:09:41.290 --> 01:09:43.290
- There's only so many tiny little twigs

01:09:43.290 --> 01:09:45.230
you find on the paddock right there

01:09:45.230 --> 01:09:47.133
where it's like, "Yes, this will do."

01:09:47.133 --> 01:09:49.130
- I remember there was a
tree next to where I pissed,

01:09:49.130 --> 01:09:50.690
and I picked up the leaves.

01:09:50.690 --> 01:09:53.936
I'm just like, "Okay, these
dead leaves, these'll work.

01:09:53.936 --> 01:09:56.117
And they gave us like
a microsecond of heat

01:09:56.117 --> 01:10:00.023
and that felt, to me at the
time, like a full 10 minutes.

01:10:02.353 --> 01:10:05.540
We start using the beer packaging.

01:10:05.540 --> 01:10:08.340
So we had all the cardboard packaging,

01:10:08.340 --> 01:10:11.380
and we went from using the beer cardboard

01:10:11.380 --> 01:10:15.640
to rationing it as a big burst of heat,

01:10:15.640 --> 01:10:17.757
because that would give us
a nice big burst of heat

01:10:17.757 --> 01:10:19.090
for a good five seconds.

01:10:19.090 --> 01:10:20.697
We'd all crowd around, and we were like,

01:10:20.697 --> 01:10:21.630
"All right, boys,

01:10:21.630 --> 01:10:23.580
when do we do the next one?
When do we use the next one?"

01:10:23.580 --> 01:10:25.420
- [Joey] I can't wait new longer.

01:10:25.420 --> 01:10:28.770
- We eventually just didn't
even drink the beers.

01:10:28.770 --> 01:10:30.860
But we'd still unwrap the beers

01:10:30.860 --> 01:10:33.100
with all the packaging so
we could use it as fuel.

01:10:33.100 --> 01:10:35.930
- Should have recycled,
but instead we needed heat.

01:10:35.930 --> 01:10:37.529
- Yeah.

01:10:37.529 --> 01:10:41.220
- Eventually, we ran
out of things to burn.

01:10:41.220 --> 01:10:42.053
I know guys, I know.

01:10:42.053 --> 01:10:42.940
- Ran outta resources.

01:10:42.940 --> 01:10:45.670
- So I put Garnt in the fire.

01:10:45.670 --> 01:10:47.143
Unfortunately, he didn't burn.

01:10:48.230 --> 01:10:50.070
- So eventually, that was when we realized

01:10:50.070 --> 01:10:51.623
we had to go to bed,

01:10:53.830 --> 01:10:56.062
and so we packed everything up.

01:10:56.062 --> 01:10:57.633
- At this point, it was
maybe around -1, -2.

01:10:58.821 --> 01:11:00.040
It was starting to get
pretty fucking cold.

01:11:00.040 --> 01:11:02.280
- It was starting to
get pretty, really cold.

01:11:02.280 --> 01:11:03.113
Yeah.

01:11:03.113 --> 01:11:04.723
It was negative degrees.

01:11:05.560 --> 01:11:07.230
For you Americans, that's negative

01:11:07.230 --> 01:11:09.510
from the freezing point of water.

01:11:09.510 --> 01:11:11.760
So I dunno what the
freezing point of water

01:11:11.760 --> 01:11:13.938
is in Fahrenheit, but it was below that.

01:11:13.938 --> 01:11:14.855
- Below 32.

01:11:15.771 --> 01:11:16.788
- Why is it 32?

01:11:16.788 --> 01:11:18.380
- 'Cause 32 Fahrenheit
is zero degrees Celsius.

01:11:18.380 --> 01:11:19.213
- Oh, okay.

01:11:19.213 --> 01:11:20.340
- I didn't know that.

01:11:20.340 --> 01:11:21.508
- I did not know that.

01:11:21.508 --> 01:11:23.006
- That's 30-something. I'm pretty sure.

01:11:23.006 --> 01:11:23.839
- Yeah.

01:11:23.839 --> 01:11:24.990
- 33.8 is one degree Celsius.

01:11:24.990 --> 01:11:28.484
- Yeah, 32 is zero. So it
was maybe 28 Fahrenheit.

01:11:28.484 --> 01:11:29.317
- Yeah.

01:11:29.317 --> 01:11:32.350
- This is when we came to realize how cold

01:11:32.350 --> 01:11:34.910
it really was going to be,

01:11:34.910 --> 01:11:36.900
without even like a piddly little fire

01:11:36.900 --> 01:11:38.530
to heat us up.

01:11:38.530 --> 01:11:42.300
I remember me and Connor
went into our tent first.

01:11:42.300 --> 01:11:45.240
So me and Connor slept
with Nabi in his tent,

01:11:45.240 --> 01:11:48.150
and Joey and Luke slept in another tent,

01:11:48.150 --> 01:11:50.568
and everyone else slept in a third tent.

01:11:50.568 --> 01:11:53.505
I remember Connor jumping in,

01:11:53.505 --> 01:11:56.250
Connor jumping in and just realizing

01:11:56.250 --> 01:11:59.923
how flimsy and thin his sleeping bag was.

01:12:01.060 --> 01:12:03.870
- I literally had gotten a raincoat

01:12:03.870 --> 01:12:06.111
that had just been fashioned into...

01:12:06.111 --> 01:12:08.550
It didn't have any padding or anything.

01:12:08.550 --> 01:12:10.127
I was like, "This is what I get

01:12:10.127 --> 01:12:13.020
for ordering the only
available sleeping bag

01:12:13.020 --> 01:12:14.410
on Amazon next-day delivery."

01:12:14.410 --> 01:12:16.530
'Cause I remember it was like 20 bucks.

01:12:16.530 --> 01:12:17.500
- 'Cause I remember,

01:12:17.500 --> 01:12:21.330
I think the warmest coat you have, by far,

01:12:21.330 --> 01:12:24.430
is your Drip jacket, and
this was the one time

01:12:24.430 --> 01:12:27.550
in the past month I've seen
you without the Drip jacket.

01:12:27.550 --> 01:12:30.520
It would've been very,
very useful, I imagine.

01:12:30.520 --> 01:12:32.160
- The reason I didn't bring it

01:12:32.160 --> 01:12:34.457
is because we were gonna
be burning a lot of shit.

01:12:34.457 --> 01:12:37.230
I didn't want to get this nice jacket

01:12:37.230 --> 01:12:38.810
that I paid way too much money for

01:12:38.810 --> 01:12:42.258
super-smoky, and it's
probably gonna be messy, too.

01:12:42.258 --> 01:12:44.730
It was messy. I dropped
condiments everywhere.

01:12:44.730 --> 01:12:46.710
I was like, "I'm gonna just leave that."

01:12:46.710 --> 01:12:47.543
- Yeah.

01:12:47.543 --> 01:12:48.376
- Which I did.

01:12:49.240 --> 01:12:51.220
- So Connor gets in this sleeping bag

01:12:51.220 --> 01:12:53.550
and soon realizes that
this is not going to be

01:12:53.550 --> 01:12:54.650
warm enough at all.

01:12:54.650 --> 01:12:55.720
He just gets over and he's like,

01:12:55.720 --> 01:12:58.747
"Garnt? Garnt, I give you
permission to cuddle me.

01:12:58.747 --> 01:13:00.540
Garnt, we are cuddling tonight."

01:13:00.540 --> 01:13:01.900
I'm like, "Connor, we
don't need to cuddle.

01:13:01.900 --> 01:13:03.298
We don't need to cuddle."

01:13:03.298 --> 01:13:05.097
Then I get into the tent and I realize,

01:13:05.097 --> 01:13:07.647
"Fuck! We're gonna need to
cuddle tonight, Connor."

01:13:10.730 --> 01:13:12.919
I take off my jackets,
as fast as possible,

01:13:12.919 --> 01:13:15.810
then I try to get in the sleeping bag,

01:13:15.810 --> 01:13:17.625
I zip myself up. I'm fucking freezing.

01:13:17.625 --> 01:13:20.530
I saw like two caterpillars
next to each other,

01:13:20.530 --> 01:13:22.905
like cuddling with each other.

01:13:22.905 --> 01:13:24.100
Then that still isn't warm enough.

01:13:24.100 --> 01:13:26.540
So I realized pretty soon

01:13:26.540 --> 01:13:28.640
that I have to sleep with my jacket on,

01:13:28.640 --> 01:13:30.300
because even with my jacket off,

01:13:30.300 --> 01:13:31.814
it's not going to be warm enough.

01:13:31.814 --> 01:13:36.520
Luckily at this point, I
think we were pretty drunk.

01:13:36.520 --> 01:13:38.730
- I must say it was very
funny from the other tent,

01:13:38.730 --> 01:13:41.730
when all I could hear was,
"Garnt, cuddle me, now!

01:13:41.730 --> 01:13:42.610
Get closer to me."

01:13:42.610 --> 01:13:44.087
- I was freezing!

01:13:44.087 --> 01:13:47.110
- "I'll be the small spoon.
You be the large spoon.

01:13:47.110 --> 01:13:48.030
Cuddle me now."

01:13:48.030 --> 01:13:50.240
- Listen, I've seen enough
Bear Grylls to know what to do.

01:13:50.240 --> 01:13:51.073
I knew what I needed to do.

01:13:51.073 --> 01:13:52.554
- Drink my piss, now.

01:13:52.554 --> 01:13:55.850
- Garnt, don't ask what I
fashioned my jeans into,

01:13:55.850 --> 01:13:56.850
a piss filter.

01:13:56.850 --> 01:13:59.030
I'm already drinking the
water. You can go next.

01:13:59.030 --> 01:13:59.863
I've already pissed in it, Garnt.

01:13:59.863 --> 01:14:02.720
- I think I was wearing
like maybe six layers.

01:14:02.720 --> 01:14:05.550
I had fashioned my spare T-shirt

01:14:05.550 --> 01:14:08.240
and put it around my
neck as a neck warmer,

01:14:08.240 --> 01:14:10.470
'cause it was so cold.

01:14:10.470 --> 01:14:12.280
- Okay. What was the
coldest part of your body?

01:14:12.280 --> 01:14:13.830
What was the part that was hurting?

01:14:13.830 --> 01:14:14.790
- [Both] My feet.

01:14:14.790 --> 01:14:15.940
- It was the feet for me, too.

01:14:15.940 --> 01:14:17.230
- I was wearing the FamilyMart socks;

01:14:17.230 --> 01:14:18.078
that didn't help.

01:14:18.078 --> 01:14:19.045
(Garnt laughing)

01:14:19.045 --> 01:14:22.740
- The one time I was like,
"Dammit, FamilyMart!"

01:14:22.740 --> 01:14:24.220
- I was rocking a pair of jeans.

01:14:24.220 --> 01:14:26.140
It was fine. My legs were fine, honestly.

01:14:26.140 --> 01:14:27.070
That wasn't the problem.

01:14:27.070 --> 01:14:29.560
I was wearing a long-sleeve thin shirt

01:14:29.560 --> 01:14:31.726
with a big thick jumper.

01:14:31.726 --> 01:14:34.640
I went to bed using my coat as the pillow,

01:14:34.640 --> 01:14:35.930
'cause I didn't have a pillow.

01:14:35.930 --> 01:14:38.540
Woke up at like 2 AM, I
think, 'cause I was too cold.

01:14:38.540 --> 01:14:41.107
I put the jacket on. It
didn't really help much,

01:14:41.107 --> 01:14:43.266
'cause by that point,
I was already too cold.

01:14:43.266 --> 01:14:44.210
- Like the jacket was ice cold.

01:14:44.210 --> 01:14:46.093
- I feel like... 'Cause I was too cold.

01:14:46.093 --> 01:14:48.520
I wasn't heating up at
all. So it didn't help.

01:14:48.520 --> 01:14:50.070
But my feet were just too cold.

01:14:51.110 --> 01:14:53.517
- The tactic that I had in my head was,

01:14:53.517 --> 01:14:56.267
"Fall asleep before you really
start feeling the cold."

01:14:58.136 --> 01:14:59.040
Before we went to the tent,

01:14:59.040 --> 01:15:01.300
we just started downing
beers like nobody's business.

01:15:01.300 --> 01:15:03.810
We're just like, "All right,
can we down a little bit?

01:15:03.810 --> 01:15:06.980
Let's do it. We fucking need
it to sleep in this weather."

01:15:06.980 --> 01:15:08.480
So after we downed the beers

01:15:08.480 --> 01:15:11.740
and I start cuddling Connor, it worked.

01:15:11.740 --> 01:15:13.510
I fell asleep actually pretty fast.

01:15:13.510 --> 01:15:14.343
- Yeah. Me too.

01:15:14.343 --> 01:15:16.397
I got a good amount of sleep, I think.

01:15:16.397 --> 01:15:18.730
Not good quality sleep. I felt rough.

01:15:18.730 --> 01:15:21.830
- I got like a good three,
four hours of sleep,

01:15:21.830 --> 01:15:24.540
because I remember waking
up at around 4:00 AM

01:15:24.540 --> 01:15:27.290
and that's when it
really started to hit me,

01:15:27.290 --> 01:15:29.084
the fucking situation I was in.

01:15:29.084 --> 01:15:30.810
- That was like the minimum.

01:15:30.810 --> 01:15:33.070
- What time did you wake
up? Or did you sleep at all?

01:15:33.070 --> 01:15:35.520
- I slept. I probably slept
maybe about two or three hours.

01:15:35.520 --> 01:15:39.550
Then I woke up around
four-ish, the same thing.

01:15:39.550 --> 01:15:41.580
Then I was just like, "I can't. I can't."

01:15:41.580 --> 01:15:44.120
I checked the temperature.
It was like -6 Celsius,

01:15:44.120 --> 01:15:45.434
and I was like, "Fuck it."

01:15:45.434 --> 01:15:48.320
- I know that if you wake up

01:15:48.320 --> 01:15:50.097
and you actually start looking
at your phone and stuff,

01:15:50.097 --> 01:15:51.460
you will wake up.

01:15:51.460 --> 01:15:52.293
So every time I woke up,

01:15:52.293 --> 01:15:54.710
'cause I woke up sporadically
throughout the night,

01:15:54.710 --> 01:15:57.620
I would just immediately force
myself to not do anything

01:15:57.620 --> 01:15:58.740
and go back to sleep.

01:15:58.740 --> 01:16:02.810
So I got to sleep until around
5:56, I think I remember.

01:16:02.810 --> 01:16:04.193
- Yeah. I got out of the tent.

01:16:04.193 --> 01:16:05.502
I was like, "I need to start walking."

01:16:05.502 --> 01:16:07.833
- At that point, it was like
four or five degrees minus.

01:16:07.833 --> 01:16:10.110
- I got out at like 4:00 AM,

01:16:10.110 --> 01:16:11.920
with all the clothes I had on,

01:16:11.920 --> 01:16:13.635
I was just like, "I'm
just gonna go for a walk,

01:16:13.635 --> 01:16:15.430
'cause this is the only
way I can be warmer."

01:16:15.430 --> 01:16:17.083
- I was so much colder walking.

01:16:17.083 --> 01:16:17.916
- Really?

01:16:17.916 --> 01:16:19.600
- Yeah. 'Cause I had the
problem of what happens

01:16:19.600 --> 01:16:21.843
when you drink too many
beers. You need to pee.

01:16:21.843 --> 01:16:22.676
- Right.

01:16:22.676 --> 01:16:24.407
- So I really, really needed to pee,

01:16:24.407 --> 01:16:26.590
and the toilet was very far away.

01:16:26.590 --> 01:16:27.470
So I walked to it.

01:16:27.470 --> 01:16:29.210
I bought a coffee from
a vending machine there

01:16:29.210 --> 01:16:33.470
to heat my hands up, 'cause
I paid a dollar every time

01:16:33.470 --> 01:16:34.610
I needed to heat my hands up.

01:16:34.610 --> 01:16:36.100
I was so cold. I didn't even give a shit.

01:16:36.100 --> 01:16:37.010
I didn't drink the coffee.

01:16:37.010 --> 01:16:38.340
I just used it as a hand warmer.

01:16:38.340 --> 01:16:42.005
So it was saving my life.
It was like my lifeline.

01:16:42.005 --> 01:16:45.130
I had gone through four
of these $1 coffees

01:16:45.130 --> 01:16:46.560
just to keep my hands warm.

01:16:46.560 --> 01:16:49.994
'Cause I was like (shivering noises)

01:16:49.994 --> 01:16:50.827
- Oh my God.

01:16:50.827 --> 01:16:51.660
- It was awful.

01:16:51.660 --> 01:16:52.493
It was dreadful.

01:16:52.493 --> 01:16:54.900
I had to go pee, and peeing was painful.

01:16:54.900 --> 01:16:58.450
But luckily, the one saving
grace of this campsite,

01:16:58.450 --> 01:17:02.660
was at the toilet,
thank God, praise Japan,

01:17:02.660 --> 01:17:04.970
had a bidet, and bidets have a heated...

01:17:04.970 --> 01:17:05.910
- Heated toilet seat!

01:17:05.910 --> 01:17:08.280
- So I sat on this toilet
seat for a good 15 minutes.

01:17:08.280 --> 01:17:09.417
Just going (sighs blissfully),

01:17:12.590 --> 01:17:14.750
just knowing that my ass
was being warmed gracefully.

01:17:14.750 --> 01:17:15.583
It was amazing.

01:17:15.583 --> 01:17:17.830
- One of my biggest fears
when it goes to camping,

01:17:17.830 --> 01:17:22.310
is having to pee after
everyone's fallen asleep.

01:17:22.310 --> 01:17:25.003
Because I have the smallest
bladder in the world.

01:17:25.003 --> 01:17:25.836
- Getting out of the tent.

01:17:25.836 --> 01:17:29.744
- Getting out of the tent
and waking everyone up

01:17:29.744 --> 01:17:33.111
as you are going out the tent to pee,

01:17:33.111 --> 01:17:34.040
which sometimes, if I'm drinking,

01:17:34.040 --> 01:17:35.423
I need to pee two or three times.

01:17:35.423 --> 01:17:36.875
- Next time, I'm just
bringing adult diapers.

01:17:36.875 --> 01:17:37.708
(Joey laughs)

01:17:37.708 --> 01:17:39.820
- Yeah. Luckily, they'll
warm you up, honestly.

01:17:39.820 --> 01:17:41.990
- I'm bringing adult diapers next time.

01:17:41.990 --> 01:17:43.060
- It would serve two things.

01:17:43.060 --> 01:17:45.780
It would warm up my legs and
my lower half of my body,

01:17:45.780 --> 01:17:48.700
and I wouldn't need to
worry about peeing anymore.

01:17:49.950 --> 01:17:51.300
- Hold on, I gotta warm up.

01:17:52.206 --> 01:17:53.730
- I actually remember,
even before we went to bed,

01:17:53.730 --> 01:17:55.410
I remember telling these boys

01:17:55.410 --> 01:17:57.310
about my irrational fears

